ken (customer-sre): We're seeing customers get duplicated side-effects when our SDK retries POSTs. They hit a 429, SDK retries, then the backend reports two executions. Repro below.

maria (support): ugh. thanks — can you paste the small repro + versions?

ken (customer-sre): Node 18.15.0, undici 5.13.0, redwood-inference-js 0.8.1
Code (minimal):
```js
const controller = new AbortController();
async function send() {
  // wrapper retries on 429 using same controller each attempt
  for (let i=0;i<3;i++){
    try {
      const res = await fetch(API_URL, {method:'POST', body:JSON.stringify(body), headers, signal: controller.signal});
      return await res.json();
    } catch (err) {
      if (isRetryable(err)) await backoff(i);
      else throw err;
    }
  }
}
```

jon (eng-oncall): Reusing the same AbortController across retries is a known footgun. If the controller gets aborted by a cancellation timeout after the first attempt, subsequent attempts inherit that aborted signal and will fail immediately or behave unpredictably. Also undici's connection pooling + streaming can keep sockets alive and combine with an abort to produce duplicate retries in some setups.

ali (dev-rel): try creating a new AbortController per attempt AND ensure you fully consume/close the response body before retrying. Example:
```js
for(...) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const res = await fetch(..., {signal: controller.signal});
  const text = await res.text(); // drain
  if (res.ok) return JSON.parse(text);
}
```

ken (customer-sre): gave that a try — looks better. Still saw one case where the backend logged two starts but one finish. I captured request ids: x-req-id: rq_6a7b, sdk-request: s_42

maria (support): can you also check whether the client retry policy is doubling up when receiving both 429 and network errors? We added idempotency recommendations to the docs. @jon any other mitigations?

jon (eng-oncall): Also check undici version — older undici had a pool edge-case where a socket aborted after transfer-encoding led to a queued retry. If you can upgrade undici to 5.18+ or Node 20's built-in fetch, that may avoid the behavior. If customers can't upgrade, we should add a safety to SDK: new AbortController per attempt + set `keepalive:false`? Need to confirm.

ci-bot: Filed issue #INF-723 with logs + suggested SDK patch. Assigned to eng-oncall.

maria (support): thanks @ken — will follow up with customer and propose the patch. :thumbsup:
